    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d233">

                <pb facs="0385" n="285" xml:id="pg285"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/121: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1934-06-29T17:00:00+03:00">June 29, 1934—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 30—4:27 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>162. Your 141, June 27, 7 p.m. Mr. Frederic Kuh recently visited Moscow and was
                    received twice by me and Mr. Wiley.<anchor n="13" xml:id="d233-f1"/> Neither of
                    us discussed with him any matter touching in any way on the preliminary naval
                    conversations or on American policy. It is well known that Kuh for years has
                    been in intimate contact with the Soviet Foreign Office and I suspect that the
                    activities in London attributed to him have been at the behest of
                        Litvinov<anchor n="14" xml:id="d233-f2"/> whom he saw on the eve of his
                    departure.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d233-f1" n="13" xml:id="d233-f1-content">
                <p>John C. Wiley, Counselor of Embassy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d233-f2" n="14" xml:id="d233-f2-content">
                <p>Maxim Litvinov, Soviet Commissar for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
